\chapter{Document pretty-printing and output targets}
\label{ch:57}
\index{Document pretty-printing and output targets}

Pretty-printing separates document construction from layout. Build a document from the intended content, layout choices and nesting, then render it for the selected width and output target. A formatter for a particular source language also needs parse-aware decisions about tokens and comments. The On1x formatter and the general PrettyPrint bridge therefore solve related problems at different levels.

\section{Compare width and escaping}
Render a nested sequence at a width where it fits on one line and another width that forces a break. Check indentation after the break. For a typesetting target, include a symbol that the output language treats specially and verify the intended escaped output. Avoid measuring escaped byte length as source display width. The bridge has bounded document and result representations. For a regression, preserve a layout oracle as well as semantic data: an expected single line is useful only when the chosen width and target stay part of the test.

\section{Give a formula two renderings}
Imagine a page with a numerator and denominator. Store the two operands as math atoms and construct a fraction with \OnxCodeTerm{pp\_frac}, rather than putting a slash and layout instructions in a plain text node. The first document says what the relation is; the second has already chosen a spelling. Rendering the same structured document to HTML/MathML and to LaTeX lets each target represent the fraction. To compare those results, check which operand appears in the numerator and which in the denominator. Comparing the byte strings for equality would ask different markup languages to use the same notation.

Next consider a code listing that includes a multiline string. Its surrounding block may acquire indentation when a group breaks. If the literal's internal newlines also acquire that indentation, copying the listing could change the literal's value. Use \OnxCodeTerm{pp\_verbatim} for this part: it preserves the internal line layout rather than reflowing words. It still participates in target escaping. Verbatim content does not grant permission to insert arbitrary unescaped target markup.

When a render produces no visible characters, first examine the status returned alongside the string. Both the successful empty document and a bridge failure can produce empty text. This matters even in a golden-output test: an assertion of string equality with empty text would accept a failed render as a passing case. Include status, width, target, and selected layout algorithm when describing what the expected output means.

When investigating a difference, vary only one rendering argument at a time. Changing both the target and the width obscures whether an extra newline came from layout or from the markup representation.

